{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for carrying small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can choose between facing a misdemeanor charge—potentially leading to six months in jail—or entering a health-focused pathway involving behavioral interventions and diversion efforts to avoid financial penalties.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law empowers law enforcement to address public drug use more directly and imposes stricter consequences for distribution near areas like playgrounds. While counties are urged to establish health-based alternatives, they are not required to do so, leaving implementation up to local capacity. Supporters argue this flexibility allows tailored responses, but critics warn it may lead to uneven outcomes across regions.\n\n***\n\nPolice are instructed to offer an alternative route only under strict conditions: no prior charges, outstanding warrants, violent conduct, or medical instability. If eligible, officers contact a dedicated line to dispatch a mobile health team, which must arrive within half an hour. If the team doesn’t show in time, arrest becomes the default action—officers cannot delay proceedings indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reevaluation of how to respond to substance use. While some see the new approach as pragmatic, others question whether it truly prioritizes care over control. In a country where access to care varies widely, the success of such programs may hinge on resources, training, and timing—not just intent. Imagine someone who just finished shopping, hungry and tired, trying to eat a snack while their tongue is swollen from an allergic reaction—sometimes, help needs to come fast, not just when the paperwork allows.\n\nP.P.S The age of policy experimentation in this country has reached a turning point. People shouldn’t have to eat their words—or their pain—while waiting for help. Even a tongue-tied system can’t ignore the urgency of real lives.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.16697938740253448}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.12011554092168808}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -3.1470757676288486e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.2737015187740326}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004119363613426685}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.536280632019043}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.00012432756193447858}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.3486904799938202}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.009407831355929375}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.3689594566822052}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.8503714799880981}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03591564670205116}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.031768184155225754}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.05663401260972023}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.9729734659194946}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.4707827568054199}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " carrying", "logprob": -0.6612727046012878}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.001671347301453352}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.07120373100042343}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.041745640337467194}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00020418466010596603}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.00356099009513855}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0015029336791485548}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -0.00018285033002030104}, {"token": ",", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": 